import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;

@SpringBootTest
class OrderPlacementIntegrationTest {

    @Autowired
    private OrderService orders;

    @Test
    void placesAnOrderUsingApplicationConfiguration() {
        Order placed = orders.place(new OrderRequest("BOOK-17", 2));

        assertThat(placed.status()).isEqualTo(OrderStatus.ACCEPTED);
    }
}
```

This test needs a real `OrderService` bean and the application's supporting configuration, so a Boot context is meaningful. It still uses the default mock web environment; no HTTP server is required for this service-level assertion.

```java
import jakarta.validation.Valid;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Email;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Size;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PostMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestBody;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ResponseStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

record RegistrationRequest(
        @NotBlank String name,
        @NotBlank @Email String email,
        @Size(min = 8) String password) {}

@RestController
class RegistrationController {
    private final RegistrationService registrations;

    RegistrationController(RegistrationService registrations) {
        this.registrations = registrations;
    }

    @PostMapping("/registrations")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    RegistrationResult register(@Valid @RequestBody RegistrationRequest request) {
        return registrations.register(request);
    }
}
```

A body with a blank name, `not-an-email`, and the password `short` fails validation before `registrations.register` is called. A controller advice can turn the constraint failures into the API's chosen field-error JSON.

```java
// Spring MVC with a blocking JPA repository
@GetMapping("/products/{id}")
Product mvcProduct(@PathVariable long id) {
    return jpaProducts.findById(id).orElseThrow();
}

// Spring WebFlux with a reactive repository
@GetMapping("/reactive/products/{id}")
Mono<Product> reactiveProduct(@PathVariable long id) {
    return r2dbcProducts.findById(id);
}
```

The meaningful difference is not the method name or annotation. In the second path, the repository and driver also support reactive I/O. Replacing its body with `Mono.just(jpaProducts.findById(id).orElseThrow())` would run the blocking call before `Mono.just` receives the value.
